---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Related from "../../components/Related.astro";
import { getSnapshot, coldStart, OI_NOTIONAL_FLOOR } from "../../lib/hyperliquid.ts";
import { pct, usd } from "../../lib/funding.ts";

/**
 * THE OPEN-INTEREST EXPLAINER.
 *
 * WHY IT IS SEPARATE FROM /open-interest. That page is a table: it shows the book and assumes
 * the reader knows what the column means. The two questions people actually ask of the number
 * — "is rising open interest bullish" and "why is your figure a fifth of the one I saw
 * elsewhere" — are answered nowhere on this site, and the second one is the difference between
 * a reader trusting a page and closing it.
 *
 * THE FRAGMENTATION PARAGRAPH IS THE POINT OF THE PAGE. A visitor who compares our BTC open
 * interest against an aggregator's sees a number roughly a fifth the size and concludes the
 * site is broken. It is not broken; it is one venue's book, stated as such. That explanation
 * exists on /open-interest and on /about as a clause; here it gets the space to actually
 * land, because it is the single most likely reason a reader distrusts this site's numbers.
 */
const snap = await getSnapshot((Astro.locals as any)?.runtime?.env?.SNAPSHOT, import.meta.env.DEV);
if (!snap.available) return coldStart();

const perps = [...snap.perps];
const totalOi = perps.reduce((s, p) => s + p.oiNotional, 0);
const totalVol = perps.reduce((s, p) => s + p.dayNtlVlm, 0);
const btc = perps.find((p) => p.symbol === "BTC") ?? perps[0];
/* THE SENTENCE SAID "LARGEST" AND THE EXPRESSION SAID "BTC". The paragraph below prints "The
   largest book covered today is {btc.symbol}, at {usd(btc.oiNotional)}" from the lookup above,
   which finds BTC by NAME and falls back to perps[0] only when BTC is absent. It has been
   accidentally true for as long as BTC has held the largest book, and would go quietly false
   the first day it does not — the failure mode this codebase keeps finding, where a superlative
   is asserted by a label and never computed. `perps` is a plain copy of snap.perps here, not a
   sorted one, so the maximum is taken explicitly rather than assumed from position. */
const biggest = perps.length
  ? perps.reduce((a, b) => (b.oiNotional > a.oiNotional ? b : a))
  : null;

/* Fastest-churning books: volume as a multiple of the position base. A different sort from
   /open-interest, which leads with size — this page is about what the ratio means. */
const churn = perps
  .filter((p) => p.oiNotional > 0 && Number.isFinite(p.dayNtlVlm))
  .sort((a, b) => b.dayNtlVlm / b.oiNotional - a.dayNtlVlm / a.oiNotional)
  .slice(0, 12);

const title = "Open interest explained: what it counts and what it cannot tell you";
const description =
  "Open interest is the value of perpetual contracts currently open — a stock, not a flow. " +
  "What rising and falling open interest imply about who is opening, what turnover measures, " +
  "and why one venue's book is not the market's.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} fetchedAt={snap.fetchedAt}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>What open interest tells you</h1>
  </div>

  <p class="callout">
    Open interest is the total value of perpetual contracts that are currently open. Every
    contract has a buyer and a seller, so it is counted once, not twice: a book with
    {" "}{usd(totalOi)} of open interest has {usd(totalOi)} of long exposure facing
    {" "}{usd(totalOi)} of short exposure, by construction. It is the size of the position
    base, not the size of the trading.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Open interest is a stock; volume is a flow</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Volume counts what changed hands over a period and resets when the period does. Open
    interest counts what is still open at this instant and carries over. The same contract can
    be traded a hundred times in a day without open interest moving at all, if every trade
    passes an existing position from one holder to another. Open interest changes only when a
    position is <em>created</em> or <em>closed</em>.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    Across the {perps.length} contracts covered here, that is {usd(totalOi)} of open interest
    against {usd(totalVol)} of volume in the last twenty-four hours — a turnover of
    {" "}{totalOi > 0 ? (totalVol / totalOi).toFixed(2) : "—"}×. A turnover above one means the
    book traded more than its own size in a day.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">The four combinations</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Open interest is most informative read together with price, because the pair distinguishes
    new positioning from unwinding. This is a reading of what has already happened, not a
    prediction of what happens next.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Price</th><th>Open interest</th><th>What is happening</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Rising</td><td>Rising</td><td>New long positions are being opened. Fresh money is taking the direction the price is going.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Falling</td><td>Rising</td><td>New short positions are being opened. Fresh money is taking the other side.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rising</td><td>Falling</td><td>Shorts are closing. The move is being driven by exits rather than by new conviction — a squeeze rather than an accumulation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Falling</td><td>Falling</td><td>Longs are closing. Positions are being unwound, which is what the tail of a liquidation cascade looks like.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="section-note" style="margin-top:10px">
    Each row describes the dominant flow, not the only one. Both sides are always present.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Why our figure is smaller than the one you saw elsewhere</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    This is the most common reason to distrust a number on this site, so it is worth stating
    plainly: <strong>every open-interest figure here is Hyperliquid's book alone.</strong> It is
    the venue this project collects from directly, and it is a minority of perpetual open
    interest across the market. Measured on 18 August 2026, Hyperliquid held $2.68B of BTC open
    interest against $6.83B on Binance and $3.91B on Bybit — roughly a fifth of those three
    together, and those three are not the whole market either.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    An aggregator adding a dozen venues will therefore report several times our figure for the
    same coin, and both numbers can be right. The difference is scope, and it is stated on
    every page that carries one. Funding is the exception: three venues publish comparable
    funding rates, so <a class="link" href="/funding">the funding pages</a> compare all three.
    {" "}<a class="link" href="/data-sources">What is collected, and what is not →</a>
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">What the number cannot tell you</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>A reading you may have seen</th><th>Why it does not follow</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>"Open interest is high, so the market is heavily long"</td>
          <td>It cannot be. Longs and shorts are equal by construction — the figure says how large the position base is, never which way it leans. Funding is the number that leans.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>"Open interest is the money at risk"</td>
          <td>It is notional value, not collateral. A book of {usd(totalOi)} held at ten times leverage is backed by a tenth of that in margin.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>"Rising open interest is bullish"</td>
          <td>Only with the price direction attached — see the table above. Rising open interest into a falling price is new shorts.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>"A drop in open interest means people left the market"</td>
          <td>Or that they were closed out. A liquidation cascade reduces open interest exactly as an orderly exit does, and the two mean opposite things.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 class="section">Turnover: how fast the book is churning</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Dividing twenty-four-hour volume by open interest gives a ratio this site prints as
    turnover. A low number means positions are being held; a high number means the same
    exposure is changing hands repeatedly. It is the cleanest available separation between a
    contract that is being <em>invested in</em> and one that is being <em>traded</em>.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Coin</th><th class="num">Turnover (24h volume ÷ OI)</th><th class="num">Open interest <span class="thscope">Hyperliquid</span></th><th class="num">Funding, annualised</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {churn.map((p) => (
          <tr>
            <td><a href={`/funding/${p.symbol.toLowerCase()}`}>{p.symbol}</a></td>
            <td class="num">{(p.dayNtlVlm / p.oiNotional).toFixed(2)}×</td>
            <td class="num">{usd(p.oiNotional)}</td>
            <td class="num">{pct(p.hlApr)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="section-note" style="margin-top:10px">
    The twelve fastest-churning covered books right now.
    {" "}<a class="link" href="/open-interest">All {perps.length}, sorted by size →</a>
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Which contracts appear here at all</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Coverage is decided by open interest and nothing else: a Hyperliquid perpetual is covered
    once its open interest clears {usd(OI_NOTIONAL_FLOOR)}, and it is retired when it falls
    materially below that and stays there. No venue, listing or partnership influences the set —
    there are none. A contract that leaves coverage answers
    {" "}<a class="link" href="/retired">410 Gone</a> rather than quietly redirecting, so a
    crawler is told the page is over rather than left guessing.
    {biggest && (
      <span>{" "}The largest book covered today is {biggest.symbol}, at {usd(biggest.oiNotional)}.</span>
    )}
  </p>

  <Related
    title="Where to go next"
    items={[
      { href: "/open-interest", label: "Open interest table", note: "Every covered contract, with 24-hour volume and turnover." },
      { href: "/learn/funding-rate", label: "Funding rates, explained", note: "The number that says which way positioning leans, which this one cannot." },
      { href: "/learn/liquidation-heatmap", label: "Liquidation heatmaps", note: "What open interest becomes when it is distributed across price levels." },
      { href: "/data-sources", label: "Data sources", note: "Which venue every figure comes from, and the coverage floor." },
      { href: "/coins", label: "Coins", note: "Price, funding and open interest per coin, one page each." },
      { href: "/learn", label: "All explainers", note: "Funding, liquidation maps, liquidation price and open interest." },
    ]}
  />

  <style>
    h2.section { margin-top: 32px; }
  </style>
</Base>
